window.docContents[91].resolve({"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L0-___-The-black-box/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L0-___-The-black-box--Small-rigorous-improvement":{"contents":"At this level, treat the oscillator as one black box with one body, one ideal linear spring, a fixed wall, and a line of motion. The body is assumed homogeneous, rigid, and point-like; those assumptions are revisited at L3.\n\n* Question* Context change* Attribute change* Risk shown* PhysLib stage\n* Which quotient is the oscillator's angular frequency?* One oscillator, one body and spring.* Mass, stiffness, angular frequency.* Bare reals accept both √(k/m) and the mistaken √(m/k).* Stages 0–1; HarmonicOscillator Basic and WithDim.\n* What does a dimension tag check?* Keep the same black box.* Potential energy carries the product dimension.* WithDim rejects assigning m/k to k/m's dimension, but does not identify kinds.* PhysLib HarmonicOscillatorWithDim.\n* What does it not see?* Same one system.* Frequency and angular frequency share T⁻¹.* PhysLib Frequency has no kind distinction.* PhysLib Optics.Frequency; ISO 80000-3.\n* Which properties are these?* Record the measurement convention.* Frequency and angular frequency become separate kinds.* Same dimension does not imply same kind.* ForPhysLib Kinds and Metrology.\n\nAt L0 the bearer stays informal: the System-context card section names one body, while the code uses values such as Quantity k ℝ. With only one of each part, no two bearers can yet be confused. L1 is where the bearer enters the type.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L0 — The black box","header":"5.1.5. Small rigorous improvement","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L0-___-The-black-box/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L0-___-The-black-box--Small-rigorous-improvement"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#hcp-l6":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols","header":"5.7. L6 — What must hold","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#hcp-l6"}});